\cf0 	Missions are the crown jewel of the Override datafile, as well as the largest and most complex resources in the game. Each misn resource corresponds to a single mission that the player can undertake, with the name of the mission (which the player sees in the mission list) being the name of the associated misn resource. The first six fields in a misn resource help Override determine where and when the mission is available:\
\
AvailStel		Which stellar objects (i.e. planets) the mission is available at\
\cf2 	-1			Any inhabited stellar\cf0 \
	128-1627		ID number of a specific stellar\
\cf2 	5000-5999		Stellar in a system adjacent to specific system\cf0 \
	9999-10127		Specific govt's stellar\
	\cf2 15000-15127	Specific g\'9avt's ally's stellar\cf0 \
	20000-20127	Stellar of anybody but this specific g\'9avt\
	\cf2 25000-25127	Specific g\'9avt's enemy's stellar\cf0 \
\
AvailBitSet		Which one of the mission flag bits, (see below) if any, must be set for this mission to become available\
\cf2 	-1		ignored\cf0 \
	0-511		this mission bit must be set\
\cf2 	1000-1511	this mission bit must be clear (functions similarly to AvailBitClear, described below)\cf0 \
\
AvailLoc		Where on a planet this mission is available\
\cf2 	0		From the mission computer\cf0 \
	1		In the bar\
	\cf2 2		Offered from ship (must set up associated p\'91rs resource as well)\cf0 \
\
AvailRecord		What your legal record in this system must be for this mission to become available\
\cf2 	0		ignored\cf0 \
	pos. value   record must be at least this high\
	\cf2 neg. value   record must be at least this low\cf0 \
	-32000	when the player has dominated the stellar in question\
	\cf2 -32001	when the player has dominated at least one stellar\cf0 \
\
AvailRating		What your combat rating must be for this mission to be available.\
	-1		ignored\
	0+		rating must be at least this high\
\
\cf2 AvailRandom	A randomization factor, to ensure that some missions aren't available all the time. Mission randomizing values are recalculated each time you warp into a system.\
	100		always available\
	1-99		available this % of the time\cf0 \
\
\
	A quick word on mission bits: Override stores 256 flags that can be set by your missions when they fail or succeed. The mission bits can then be checked to see whether a mission is allowed to be available. There are three uses for this:\
\
1. Ensuring that a mission is only available once.\
2. Creating branching plotlines, in which the types of missions offered depend\
     on your past successes and failuires.\
3. Creating a number of missions that are available at the same time, but which\
     are mutually exclusive. (e.g. you wouldn't want the player to go on more\
     that one of a set of three missions) These missions could be made\
     unavailable if bit 37 was set, for example, and then would set bit 37 on\
     completion.\
\
\
The next two fields in the misn resource define where the player needs to go to complete the mission:\
\
TravelStel		Which stellar object the player must go to during the mission\
\cf2 	-1			No specific stellar destination\cf0 \
	-2			A random inhabited stellar\
	\cf2 -3			A random uninhabited planet\cf0 \
	128-1627		ID number of a specific stellar\
	\cf2 9999-10127		Random stellar of a specific g\'9avt\cf0 \
	15000-15127	Random stellar of a specific g\'9avt's ally\
	\cf2 20000-20127	Random stellar of anybody but this specific g\'9avt\cf0 \
	25000-25127	Random stellar of specific g\'9avt's enemy\
\
ReturnStel		Where the player must return to in order to complete the mission and receive payment\
\cf2 	-1			No specific stellar destination\cf0 \
	-2			A random inhabited stellar\
\cf2 	-3			A random uninhabited stellar\
\cf0 	-4			The initial stellar, where the mission was accepted\
	\cf2 128-1627		ID number of a specific stellar\cf0 \
	10000-10127	Random stellar of a specific g\'9avt\
	\cf2 15000-15127	Random stellar of a specific g\'9avt's ally\cf0 \
	20000-20127	Random stellar of anybody but this specific g\'9avt\
	\cf2 25000-25127	Random stellar of specific g\'9avt's enemy\cf0 \
\
\
The next five fields tell Override about any special cargo associated with a mission:\
\
CargoType		What type of cargo must be carried\
\cf2 	-1		No special cargo for this mission\cf0 \
	0-63		Specific cargo type\
\cf2 	1000		Random cargo of types 0-5 (the standard types)\cf0 \
\
CargoQty		What amount of cargo must be carried\
\cf2 	-1		Ignored (no cargo)\cf0 \
	0 and up	This many tons of cargo\
\cf2 	-2 and below abs(CargoQty) tons, \'b1 50%\cf0 \
\
PickupMode		Where the cargo is to be picked up\
	\cf2 -1		Ignored\
\cf0 	0		Pick up at mission start\
	\cf2 1		Pick up at TravelStel\cf0 \
	2		Pick up when boarding special ship\
\
DropOffMode	Where the cargo is to be dropped off [Note: don't set your cargo to be picked up and dropped off at the same place, as it may cause Override to behave strangely]\
\cf2 	-1	Ignored\cf0 \
	0	Drop off at TravelStel\
	\cf2 1	Drop off at mission end (ReturnStel)\cf0 \
\
ScanGovt		Which government considers your cargo illegal\
	-1		Ignored\
	128-255	ID number of a government that considers this cargo illegal. If you're scanned by a ship of this government, or any government that's not its enemy (important!) you'll get that government's SmugglePenalty added to your record.\
\
FailIfScanned	Sets whether the mission fails if you're detected carrying the cargo\
	0		Mission doesn't fail if scanned\
	Nonzero	Mission fails if you're scanned\
\
\
The next field tells Override what to give you if you're successful in your mission:\
